>
>the stream class should not obtain the mutex for you since few aplications
>would need it, if thats what you want then, write a subclass of streams
>that does the locks and then just calls the normal stream methods
>

	That doesn't really solve the problem:

	cout << "This is " << "a test\n";

	Even if the inserters handle serialization themselves, there's no
guarantee that you won't get a context switch between invocations of the
inerters.  If two threads both called this code, the result could be
something like this:

	This is This is a test
	a test

	There are really two separate issues that a programmer faces when
dealing with multiple threads: making sure that individual functions can be
used safely with threads, and making sure that the program logic is correct
when using threads.  Serialization control inside the iostream library
addresses the first issue, but not the second.  Without serialization control,
this erroneous program may well crash.  With it, the program displays
nonsense. From a user's perspective, there's not a big difference between the
two results.
	-- Pete




