   You can work around it in a given project by having one of your central
h files #include the relevant definitions.  That's how we handle it, and
it usually works out OK.

   Depending on your environment, it may be very easy to catch -- you'll
hang!  ;-)  In a situation where a blocking I/O doesn't block for long,
you'll just get degraded performance.

   Since followups were redirected to comp.unix.osf.misc, I assume we're
talking in the context of OSF/1, so with kernel support for threads, things
may be different.  There are different models of how pthreads threads map
onto OSF/1 kernel threads, so it depends upon the mapping chosen.  If threads
always map one-to-one to kernel threads, I believe that you will not have a
problem.  If the mapping is not one-to-one, you can/will get one of the kernel
threads hung in a select() or other blocking I/O call.

|>
|>And, are the jacket routines also used to map blocking I/O calls (e.g.
|>read(), recvfrom()) to non-blocking versions?
|>

   Yes.  It's true of any call which blocks the running thread while in
the kernel.

