Most DCE implementations I've seen actually put thread-safe versions of
system calls and standard library functions in a library that gets linked
in with DCE. Thus, you get the "proper" versions of these functions
even if you deliberately turn off the #defines (which is sometimes
necessary).

Yes, all this #defining and re-defining is a mess, and I hope it gets easier
when more vendors offer user-accessible threads in the OS. Right now,
all this is one reason why the toughest thing about DCE programming is
writing the makefile.

