Well there are tools and there are tools, but IMHO the best tool you
need to tune any kernel is 'know_how', that comes from studying how
the kernel works and understand it.

Programs like tunesh for SCO are fine, but I think you should do a bit
of learning first if you are going to attack tuning the kernel.

Read Maurice Bach's "The Design of the UNIX operating system" and when
you understand the book, you'll understand the kernel, you won't be
perfect but you'll have a better idea at what to look at.

I have notice that there is a book called "System Performance Tuning"
by Mike Loukides from O'Reilly, I have not read it but ...

As far as general system administration tools are concerned, get
yourself into Perl and download Perl scripts from a Perl archive,
you'll have all you probably will need.

HOWEVER, there is no substitute to knowing what the hell you are
doing!

I would not attempt to tune my turbo charged, electronic ingition, 
fuel injected car, nor should you your kernel.

